  "rule": "Each horizontal 7-bar on a gap row becomes 8 6 8, and 6 is also painted in its middle column on the tracks above and below, forming a vertical connector. Green markers are rerouted by an amidakuji walk. From each segment endpoint, walk inward. At the nearest rung, cross to the other track and reverse direction. Stop at the segment end when no rung remains ahead. Pairs whose ends are on the same track keep their markers. Otherwise the pair's markers are stacked inward from the lower endpoint. All other 3s become 8.",
  "scope": "Randomizes the width (10..30), the track count (4..15, height 2T-1), 0..2 gaps per track (every segment at least 3 long) and 0..3 rungs per gap row (at least max(2, T-2) in total). Colours are fixed (0 background, 8 tracks, 7 rungs, 6 connectors, 3 markers), as in the task. Markers follow the task convention: the outer ends of all tracks except the bottom one. The generator does not deliberately sample markers at inner gap endpoints, rungs longer than 3, or bars of other colours.",
  "constraints": [
    "Rung middles lie in [2, W-3] and are at least 3 columns from any gap on both adjacent tracks. They are therefore never on a gap or within 1 cell of a segment end, so stacked markers never overwrite a 6 or a 0.",
    "Bars on the same gap row are at least 4 apart, so their runs of 7 never merge.",
    "Rungs on consecutive gap rows (which share a track) are at least 2 columns apart. This gives distinct columns and no ties in the walk.",
    "Segments are at least 3 long, so the inward stacking cell is never another endpoint.",
    "Track rows keep a majority of 8s (W >= 10, at most 2 gaps), so the tracks are identifiable.",
    "Every scene has rungs, so the output always differs from the input."
  ],
  "known_limitations": [
    "The marker placement convention is fixed: outer ends only, and none on the bottom track.",
    "Rung bars are always exactly 3 wide.",
    "Some random scenes can move few or no markers to a different row. The output still always differs from the input because the rungs are always rotated.",
    "Explicit geometry receives dimension, list-length, overlap, shared-column and endpoint checks; random sampling additionally enforces the stronger rung/gap spacing constraints.",
    "Bounded rung sampling raises if it cannot meet the requested scene constraints."
  ],
